The code statements that lead to the bug are:
cbuf = kzalloc_flex(*cbuf, bufs, size);
cbuf->size = size;
Reason: The `kzalloc_flex` function can return NULL if memory allocation fails. Immediately after the allocation, the code dereferences `cbuf` unconditionally to assign `cbuf->size`. If `cbuf` is NULL, this causes a NULL pointer dereference, leading to a kernel crash.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
cbuf = kzalloc_flex(*cbuf, bufs, size);
if (cbuf)
    cbuf->size = size;
Reason: Adding a NULL check before accessing `cbuf->size` ensures that the assignment only occurs when the memory allocation succeeded. This prevents the null pointer dereference and safely returns NULL to the caller when allocation fails.

